#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124301 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "69da2e7c-7ede-5966-8ff9-13bca51c0420" :question/math-academy-id "e-4813" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: | \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x) \\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =-f(2)-g(2) \\\\  & =-(f(2)+g(2)) \\\\  & =-(f+g)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $f+g \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x) \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =kf(0) \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-f(2)) \\\\  & =-kf(2) \\\\  & =-(kf)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $kf \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "7fed39de-7304-52a0-a94d-e55703175d7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8233" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R},$ and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0.$ Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$ This matrix belongs to $N,$  and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0.$ If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0.$ This matrix does not belong to $N,$  and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "05151ae0-102c-5ce8-8bc6-082e22aa77d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8234" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ at^3-bt \\: | \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{3}-{b}_{1}t)+({a}_{2}{t}^{3}-{b}_{2}t) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{3}-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{3}-bt) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{3}-(kb)t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a60d388-bc83-5af7-8225-3508acff2075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241407} {:answer/id #uuid "486f34a6-fce7-59c0-b718-04843afaa8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241836} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4f8115-6911-5b89-ae5f-2e6c858659ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242272} {:answer/id #uuid "d742eee0-3a02-5298-9b21-8bd95e26bb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243090} {:answer/id #uuid "e42fe48a-c999-5f78-b148-00f5fd4a9bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a60d388-bc83-5af7-8225-3508acff2075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241407} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a253ae0-6c8f-5b4c-b09b-a9105706149e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3056b610-3deb-5e11-aff8-96abed8c674b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107102" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5}+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}+b$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{5}+b) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5}+(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d872b22-7cba-5036-8273-9a20efcc7e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241729} {:answer/id #uuid "78c2ab8b-9c2d-551d-8848-61255645e2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242245} {:answer/id #uuid "7c438634-2ca9-5de8-a418-b73be0ed3bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242270} {:answer/id #uuid "eec86e1b-cd5f-56da-9600-e976237c1328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243300} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfa47ed-d690-5ca9-ab1b-37e40f90f69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec86e1b-cd5f-56da-9600-e976237c1328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243300} :answer-field/id #uuid "0be788ee-6d06-5deb-86a5-8c46bba98ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f27a4a5-4686-5577-a411-11aa97fab74a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107112" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C={[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & -ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230a6e01-18bc-58d0-8387-1d66163f631b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241484} {:answer/id #uuid "426b663e-f591-5196-99d7-5a2d5500a45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241777} {:answer/id #uuid "4db4b11e-cc15-5b26-8274-c5279317ce7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241875} {:answer/id #uuid "504d1f79-8308-5f06-a1aa-17458982e5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241897} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ef2203-b098-59db-9ad6-e52a17c8c5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504d1f79-8308-5f06-a1aa-17458982e5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241897} :answer-field/id #uuid "9183d31f-6b62-5ef0-8cb0-c498d066f789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72b2f218-4463-5051-8c3b-5d02162b5905" :question/math-academy-id "q-107115" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of all its entries equals $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {d}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {d}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D.$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & {d}_{1}+{d}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+({d}_{1}+{d}_{2}) & = \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1})+({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1}) & = \\\\ 0+0 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ also belongs to $D.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\in D$ and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ \\alpha c & \\alpha d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and so\nthe sum of the entries of $\\alpha A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a+\\alpha b+\\alpha c+\\alpha d & =\\alpha (a+b+c+d) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $D$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "809e6797-c522-5612-8bd7-cb8615d531d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123531} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c34f41-064f-5f16-aab2-eac2730d42da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123532} {:answer/id #uuid "01f80abc-829f-5786-84f6-ce1a3275419d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123533} {:answer/id #uuid "421db684-9acf-5c67-bbf1-087ecc26897f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123534} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6857c3-0a48-5ef2-9fca-96aa0ee678ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123535}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fedfc0aa-7927-5b40-97e2-4079ddb0435f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123530}] :question/id #uuid "bff3a0b0-c74a-55ac-a94b-e3a390164777" :question/math-academy-id "q-43772" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $M_{2}(R)$ of all $2 \\cdot 2$ matrices over $R$, and its subset $D$ containing all $2 \\cdot 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $1$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n2. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $D$ is a subspace of $M_{2}(R)$\n4. $D$ is **not** a subspace of $M_{2}(R)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1ffc5be-6eb8-5d79-a371-0f41d244eff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123543} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea96d09-2414-59e7-93d9-1c14b954d85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123544} {:answer/id #uuid "54836036-61d0-5040-9a35-891c3ceb0772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123545} {:answer/id #uuid "5504a5b0-7336-517a-9f6f-e3a589e714ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123546} {:answer/id #uuid "652e332d-f23d-5896-af54-53390e1a7000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123547}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd5febf-f795-5bf4-9d65-de01677ca83e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123542}] :question/id #uuid "1b6af7e1-2428-58bf-a924-9c6a5027415f" :question/math-academy-id "q-43774" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0, 1]$ of all continuous functions $f: [0, 1] → R$ and its subset $D = {f ∈ C[0, 1] \\mid f(0) = 3}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $D$ is closed under function addition\n2. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $D$ is a subspace of $C[0, 1]$\n4. $D$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0, 1]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06326135-466a-5cea-9046-55b5cffec1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326988} {:answer/id #uuid "40442251-6314-57df-aaca-6cc53942fffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327499} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecdfcc7-8411-5dbf-bf67-8ea5ca0fb879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328305} {:answer/id #uuid "a160159b-6c35-530a-9ad8-654a2c5b6853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328336} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6a79d6-167b-5ea4-984e-634f568525dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a160159b-6c35-530a-9ad8-654a2c5b6853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328336} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97d4e60-e983-5b5a-8733-b469da867546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76fb5f0d-770c-53e0-bb5c-9231d8688147" :question/math-academy-id "q-43776" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $L$ containing all invertible $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example if $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0760e94e-974d-514d-b587-d7fd3c885c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326992} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8b6873-3df2-5179-b9b5-b5510077e1df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327041} {:answer/id #uuid "23bc63bc-10b5-5f01-890c-5ba8486b87a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327244} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff1cc31-fa85-5022-b16d-f926a1325d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328037} {:answer/id #uuid "a0af5a87-cb9e-55bd-95e4-a427c9a8c027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0af5a87-cb9e-55bd-95e4-a427c9a8c027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328326} :answer-field/id #uuid "53d76603-13d4-5c61-bab7-ac9f12515d55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd839bdc-19bc-52f4-a042-3d3abcdd708c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43778" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B={p(t)\\in {R}_{3}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=1}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(1)=g(1)=1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+g(1) & =1+1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(1)=1$ and $k=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1053fe67-e452-5e43-9bb8-ae33f4d3e778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357883} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb575b8-c82d-587a-b04c-5707c757472a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358294} {:answer/id #uuid "a147d160-a156-5831-8cba-5c29b4aca2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359156} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f42a5-a427-5e69-ad2f-206dbea65336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359187} {:answer/id #uuid "fa494a1c-d0a0-58f7-a9fb-58f8e537f805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fb575b8-c82d-587a-b04c-5707c757472a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358294}